# SRG-APP-000820-WSR-000170  -  CAT II
# The web server must implement multifactor authentication for local;
# network; and/or remote access to privileged accounts; and/or nonprivileged
# accounts such that one of the factors is provided by a device separate
# from the system gaining access.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations. Edit this
# file to change how this requirement is assessed here; it is read at run
# time. Regenerating overwrites it.

id:              SRG-APP-000820-WSR-000170
group:           V-264343
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      documented
cci:             CCI-004046
control_rev5:    IA-2

contribution:    Splunk can carry a second authentication factor itself:
                 authentication.conf [authentication] externalTwoFactorAuthVendor loads a
                 supported multifactor vendor (Duo or RSA) and
                 externalTwoFactorAuthSettings points at that vendor's configuration
                 stanza, which places the second factor on a device separate from the one
                 being used to reach Splunk. The product's alternative path for the same
                 objective is a CAC/PIV certificate (web.conf enableCertBasedUserAuth,
                 requireClientCert), where the separate device is the card itself.

governs:         authentication.conf - How users sign in, and the password rules applied to local accounts.

check:           authentication.conf [authentication] externalTwoFactorAuthVendor in Duo|RSA
check:           authentication.conf [authentication] externalTwoFactorAuthSettings non_empty
# --- vendor verification: generated, do not edit by hand
corrected:       authentication.conf [authentication] externalTwoFactorAuthVendor non_empty -> authentication.conf [authentication] externalTwoFactorAuthVendor in Duo|RSA
vendor:          authentication.conf [authentication] externalTwoFactorAuthSettings https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/authentication.conf
vendor:          authentication.conf [authentication] externalTwoFactorAuthVendor https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/authentication.conf
# --- end vendor verification

external:        The separate device itself and its enrollment per user (phone for Duo
                 push, RSA token) - carried by the multifactor vendor's service and the
                 organization's enrollment procedure.
external:        Reachability of the Duo or RSA service from the instance - carried by the
                 network and the vendor's service.
external:        Where the second factor is instead a CAC/PIV card accepted by the
                 product's certificate-based authentication, or is terminated by an
                 approved SSO proxy, these native vendor settings may legitimately be empty
                 and the factor is carried by that card or proxy; the web.conf cert
                 settings observed on this requirement show which path is in use.
external:        The determination of which accounts (privileged, non-privileged, local,
                 network, remote) must be in scope - organization-defined.

citation:        splunk-spec-files/authentication.conf.spec:87
                 (externalTwoFactorAuthVendor; empty string disables multifactor
                 authentication), :95 (externalTwoFactorAuthSettings, no default);
                 splunk-spec-files/web.conf.spec:120, :151 (alternative CAC path)
finding:         Splunk Web is not configured for multifactor authentication with a factor
                 from a separate device - authentication.conf [authentication]
                 externalTwoFactorAuthVendor/externalTwoFactorAuthSettings are empty and no
                 certificate-based (CAC/PIV) or approved SSO proxy path was presented in
                 their place; this determination is from the perspective of the application
                 layer alone, the separate device and its enrollment being outside the
                 application.
